This project implements offline and online ranking algorithms to efficiently select and sort the top 10% of players from large datasets. 
The offline algorithms use heapsort with early stopping and a quickselect/quicksort hybrid to process static data in-place with optimal memory usage. 
The online algorithm maintains a min-heap of fixed size while streaming data, continuously tracking the highest-level players and recording cutoff thresholds at specified intervals. 
Key data structures include STL heap operations on raw vectors (avoiding priority queues for memory efficiency), custom player streams for data input, and iterator-based heap manipulation. 
The project demonstrates the trade-offs between offline batch processing and online streaming algorithms, with an extra credit component that fetches real-time data from a REST API using HTTP requests and JSON parsing.
